    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of León grew out of the Kingdom of Asturias, the small Christian realm founded in the mountains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> after the Muslim conquest of 711. Under Alfonso III (r. 866–910) the Asturian kings pushed south to the Douro, resettling the thinly populated plain with colonists from the north and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and fortifying towns such as Zamora, Toro and the old Roman camp of León. In 910 Alfonso III was deposed by his sons, and his realm was divided: García I took the lands south of the mountains, centred on León, Ordoño received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a> and Fruela Asturias. When García died in 914 Ordoño II reunited most of the inheritance and made León his royal seat, and historians date the kingdom's name from this transfer of the capital from Oviedo. The new kingdom thus combined the mountain core of Asturias, the populous region of Galicia in the north-west and the frontier lands of the Douro basin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the east, governed by counts, as its most exposed and increasingly independent district. The Leonese kings presented themselves as heirs of the Visigothic monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a>, a claim that gave ideological purpose to their wars against al-Andalus and to the resettlement (<em>repoblación</em>) of the lands to the south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#taifa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taifa.jpg" alt="Taifa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#taifa">Taifa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taifas2.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">No machine-readable author provided. Falconaumanni assumed (based on copyright claims)., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>León was an agrarian frontier society. Its wealth lay in grain, vines and above all livestock, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and sheep were easier to save from raids than crops. Free peasant settlers received land and privileges under charters (<em>fueros</em>) in return for settling the frontier, alongside monasteries and nobles who claimed land by <em>presura</em>, the occupation of vacant land. Society comprised nobles, clergy, free peasants and dependent cultivators, with Mozarabs, Arabic-speaking Christians who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> bringing their own artistic traditions, and Jewish communities in the towns. The city of León, seat of the court and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishop</a>, and later Astorga, Zamora, Salamanca and Santiago de Compostela were the main centres. Trade was modest until the eleventh century, when tribute in gold (<em>parias</em>) from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms of al-Andalus enriched the court and the Church, and the pilgrimage road to the shrine of Saint James at Santiago brought French pilgrims, merchants and settlers, with communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> growing up at Sahagún and León. Cluniac monks, generously endowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, reformed monastic life, and the Roman liturgy replaced the old Hispanic, or Mozarabic, rite in the 1080s. A royal coinage was first minted under Alfonso VI, a sign of the economy's growth in the eleventh century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-sagrajas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas.jpg" alt="Battle of Sagrajas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalha_de_Zalaka_-_Hist%C3%B3ria_de_Portugal,_popular_e_ilustrada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roque Gameiro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Leonese warfare combined royal campaigns with constant frontier raiding by nobles, town militias and settlers who were obliged to serve on horseback or on foot under their <em>fueros</em>. Ordoño II won a victory at San Esteban de Gormaz in 917 but was badly defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> at Valdejunquera in 920. Ramiro II (r. 931–951), allied with the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Fernán González, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> at the Battle of Simancas in 939, one of the few great Christian victories of the period, and resettled the Tormes valley around Salamanca. In the late tenth century the balance turned: al-Mansur, the chief minister of the caliphate known in Spanish as Almanzor, led dozens of campaigns into the north, sacking the city of León in the late 980s and Santiago de Compostela in 997. After the collapse of the caliphate the advantage returned to the Christians. Ferdinand I took Coimbra in 1064, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a>, the old Visigothic capital, in 1085, but the Almoravid victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a> in 1086 and the death of his heir at Uclés in 1108 checked the advance. Under Ferdinand II and Alfonso IX the frontier moved south again, helped by the military orders of Santiago and Alcántara, until Alfonso IX took Cáceres in 1229 and Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#badajoz">Badajoz</a> in 1230.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VI of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlfonsoVI_of_Castile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded by MaiDireLollo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king of León ruled with a royal court (<em>curia regia</em>) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a> and palace officials, and through counts and royal agents (<em>merinos</em>) who governed districts, collected dues and administered justice. The <em>Liber Iudiciorum</em>, the Visigothic law code, remained the basis of law, supplemented by local charters and custom. The kings stressed their descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#visigoths">Visigoths</a>, and from the tenth century some styled themselves emperor (<em>imperator</em>), a claim to precedence over the other Christian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> called himself emperor of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, and Alfonso VII was crowned emperor in León in 1135. Alfonso V's <em>fuero</em> of León, issued at a royal council in 1017 or 1020, set out rules for the city and the kingdom and became a model for later charters. The most celebrated institution was the assembly summoned by Alfonso IX in León in 1188, at the start of his reign, where representatives of the towns sat alongside bishops and nobles; the king promised to respect the laws, to seek their counsel on war and peace and to protect property. Its decrees, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a> recognised in 2013 as the oldest documentary record of the European parliamentary system, have made the Cortes of León of 1188 a landmark in the history of representative government. Alfonso IX also founded the University of Salamanca around 1218.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#seville"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seville.jpg" alt="Seville" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sevilla_Cathedral_-_Southeast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ingo Mehling, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>León carried the Asturian idea of a Christian restoration of Visigothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> into the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and its kings were the leading Christian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> for much of the tenth and eleventh centuries. Its expansion to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tagus">Tagus</a> and later the Guadiana laid foundations for the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and the County of Portugal, granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, became the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a>, recognised by León in the Treaty of Zamora in 1143. Culturally the kingdom produced some of the finest art of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. The illuminated Beatus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a>, copies of Beatus of Liébana's commentary on the Apocalypse, were painted in Leonese scriptoria in the Mozarabic style, among them the Beatus of Facundus made for Ferdinand I and Queen Sancha in 1047. The church of San Isidoro in León, which received the relics of Isidore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> in 1063, houses the Pantheon of the Kings, whose twelfth-century Romanesque paintings are among the best preserved in Europe. The pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, promoted by the Leonese kings and by Archbishop Diego Gelmírez, became one of the great routes of Christendom. The Cortes of 1188 is often cited as the earliest European parliament to include townsmen, and the Leonese language survives in the Asturian and Leonese dialects of today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa.jpg" alt="Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_las_Navas_de_Tolosa,_por_Francisco_van_Halen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco de Paula Van Halen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>León's history was bound up with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, its eastern county turned rival. Castile under Fernán González became effectively autonomous in the mid-tenth century, and in 1037 Ferdinand I, count of Castile and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a>, killed Bermudo III, the last king of the old Leonese line, at the Battle of Tamarón and took the Leonese throne by right of his wife Sancha. Ferdinand divided his kingdoms among his sons in 1065, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> reunited León, Castile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a> by 1072, and they remained together under Urraca and Alfonso VII. At Alfonso VII's death in 1157 León and Castile were separated again, Ferdinand II taking León and Sancho III Castile. For the next seventy years the two kingdoms were rivals, competing for frontier territory and sometimes allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> against each other; Alfonso IX took no part in the Castilian victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. Alfonso IX's marriage to Berenguela of Castile produced a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a>, who became king of Castile in 1217. When Alfonso IX died in 1230 he left León to his daughters by an earlier marriage, Sancha and Dulce, but the Treaty of Benavente, negotiated by their mothers, gave the kingdom to Ferdinand III, uniting León and Castile permanently in the Crown of Castile.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>León was the leading Christian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a> in the tenth and eleventh centuries, carrying the Visigothic legacy of Asturias south to the Douro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tagus">Tagus</a>, producing the Beatus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> and the Pantheon of San Isidoro, and summoning in 1188 an assembly with town representatives often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s first parliament.</em></p>

